Now Important 2: polled terminal toast. Need refactor and tests.

Let's add tests with real timers. Need understand current DatabaseTab job polling. `watchJob` starts timer 1200ms. We'll mock `getDatabaseJob` sequence. Add test:
```ts
it('toasts a job that finishes during polling', async () => {
  vi.mocked(db.createServiceBackup).mockResolvedValue(RUNNING_JOB); // running
  vi.mocked(db.getDatabaseJob)
    .mockResolvedValueOnce({...RUNNING_JOB, status:'running', progress:60})
    .mockResolvedValueOnce({...RUNNING_JOB, status:'failed', message:'disk full', backup_id:'bkp_1'});
  render...
  click Back up app;
  await waitFor(() => {
    const toast = get(toasts).find(t => t.title === 'backup failed');
    expect(toast?.kind).toBe('error');
  }, {timeout: 4000});
  expect(screen.getByText('disk full')).toBeTruthy();
});
```
But `createServiceBackup` returns RUNNING_JOB already used in existing "shows job progress and cancels..."? That test is BackupsTab. DatabaseTab has no running job test. Add constants or inline.
- The poll interval starts at 1200; first get at 1200 returns running; second at 2400 returns failed. WaitFor timeout 4000. Good.
- `finishJob` will set job, toast, stop, onRefresh. The component remains; job card shows failed.
- For canceled/succeeded, could test one more in BackupsTab. The finding asks running then failed/canceled/succeeded. Add three? Could parameterize with `it.each` but timers 2.4s each ÔåÆ 7s. Acceptable but slow. Maybe use fake timers to speed. Let's try fake timers in one test? Svelte + fake timers can be tricky. We can use `vi.useFakeTimers()` and `await vi.advanceTimersByTimeAsync(1300)` twice, then `await waitFor` might not work with fake timers. Could use `await vi.runOnlyPendingTimersAsync()` and then assert store. But Svelte state updates may need `tick`. Simpler real timers.
- Add one test in DatabaseTab for failed, one in BackupsTab for succeeded (or canceled). The audit says "Add tests where getDatabaseJob returns running then failed/canceled/succeeded." Maybe one test with `it.each(['failed','canceled','succeeded'])` in DatabaseTab, 3├ù2.4=7.2s. Full suite already 90s; acceptable. Let's do `it.each` with statuses and expected toast kind. Use one render per status. Each test 2.4s. Fine.
- Need mock `getDatabaseJob` per status; createServiceBackup returns RUNNING_JOB.
- For succeeded status, `backup_id` maybe. Toast title `backup succeeded`.
- Add `RUNNING_JOB` constant to DatabaseTab.test (currently not; has failed/canceled inline). Add.
- `afterEach` clear mocks and timers? Component cleanup clears interval. Good.
- `get(toasts)` cleanup beforeEach.

Implementation:
- Add `finishJob` in both components:
```ts
function finishJob(next: DatabaseJob): void {
  job = next;
  jobError = null;
  stopJobPolling();
  addToast({kind: jobToastKind(next.status), title: `${next.kind} ${next.status}`, message: ...});
  if ((next.status==='failed'||next.status==='canceled') && next.backup_id && (next.kind==='restore'||next.kind==='import')) {
    // persistent recovery later
  }
  onRefresh();
}
```
- `watchJob`:
```ts
function watchJob(next) {
  job = next; jobError=null;
  if (!jobRunning(next)) { finishJob(next); return; }
  stopJobPolling();
  onjobchange?.(true, next);
  jobTimer = setInterval(() => void pollJob(next.id), 1200);
}
```
- `pollJob`:
```ts
try {
 const next = await getDatabaseJob(id);
 if (generation? no) 
 if (!jobRunning(next)) { finishJob(next); return; }
 job = next; jobError=null;
} catch ...
```
- `stopJobPolling` currently calls `onjobchange?.(false)`. For Important minor "terminal new job can leave previous poll timer running", finishJob calls stop. Good.
- `cancelJob`: currently manually sets canceled, stops, toast, refresh. Could call finishJob({...job,status:'canceled'}) to avoid duplicate toast? It already adds info toast. Use finishJob to centralize; but message differs. Keep, but ensure stop.
- We'll handle active job persistence later.

Add tests first.

